The Greyhollow vale is a grey pocket cut into the cold shoulder of the mountains, where the town clings to the slope and the mine runs deep beneath it. Folk here keep time by the shift-bell and the toll of the Anvil Father's hammer; the forge-god watches over stone and steel alike, and his temple has guarded the town's dead as long as anyone remembers.

Brakka Ironvein, hill dwarf, has kept that temple's gate for six years. He wears the chain and carries the longsword of his post, and at his belt hangs the hooded lantern Hessa pressed into his hands — for what little light the late afternoon still gives will not reach the old workings.

He stands now at the mine mouth. It is a rough arch shored with old timbers, the darkness beyond complete, the cold air breathing out of it smelling of wet stone and rust. Somewhere below, three miners have been three days gone, and a fourth came up last night babbling about lights that walk, and died before dawn. At sunrise the council will brick this entrance shut, missing men or not.

What do you do?


>>>>>> round 1, the player: I check the lantern's shutter and oil, then tie a length of rope from a sound timber at the mine mouth to my belt so I can find my way back. Keeping my shield up and my longsword ready, I step into the dark and call out the miners' names, listening for any answer.

  [dice Brakka's Wisdom (Perception) check, listening for any answer: 1d20+4 = 1d20 [3] + 4 = 7]
  [time: 5 minutes later: Day 1, 5:05 PM]

The lantern is sound. The shutter slides true, and there is a flask of oil already in the well — six hours of light, should you want it. You tie the rope off to a sound timber at the mouth and let it pay out behind you, then set your shield before you, longsword ready, and step under the arch.

Beyond the mouth the mine is black, but your dwarf-eyes take it in as grey. A low gallery hewn from the rock runs on ahead, shored with old timbers whose props are furred white with mould, a rusted rail running down its centre. The air in here is colder and smells of wet stone and old rust; your breath smokes.

You call the miners' names, one after another. Your voice runs down the gallery and comes back to you flattened, and when it dies there is only the slow drip of water somewhere deeper in. No answer comes back.

The gallery slopes gently down into the dark ahead, the rope paying out behind you.


