You open the shutter a crack, but no light comes — the wick is unlit. In the grey of your darkvision the gallery is clear enough, so you go on slow along the rail, scanning the floor.

The dust is thick here, and though no one had passed this way in a generation before these last few days, the story of those men is written plainly in it. Three sets of boots go down into the dark, side by side, their steps easy. Over them, one set comes back up: the fourth man, by the look of it, his stride short and then staggering, dragging at the end as if he were running from something and then stumbling. Here and there his hand has brushed the wall.

Fifty paces in, a pick lies in the dust beside the rail, dropped and not picked up again. A little farther, a dented tin lamp, its glass broken, lies where it was thrown aside. The returning tracks pass right over it and go on.

Ahead, the gallery runs on and begins to turn, still sloping down. No light walks in the dark that you can see.


