Then you ease back to the stope for another look. The scrap of wool is coarse and grey, the stuff of a miner's shirt, torn ragged at the edge, not cut. The blood on the stones is dry and dark, and now that you look for it you make out a smear in the dust leading from the churned middle of the floor to the base of the rockfall — as if a man already bleeding crawled or was dragged to the wall. At the rocks themselves the smear ends. Whether he lies beneath them, only moving the stone would tell.


