You lay a hand on Balder's cold shoulder and murmur the old words to the Anvil Father, asking the forge-god to take up his hammer against the dark and hold Balder's soul in the warm. Then you go through his pockets.

In his belt-pouch there is a handful of silver and copper. On his belt a flask of oil, still full, and a stub of chalk for marking his way. You take his unbroken lamp, and you cover his face with the torn grey wool so the dust and the dark need not look on him any longer. Word of him you will carry back yourself.

Shield up, you leave him and follow the gallery down toward the light, the rope paying out behind you. The passage bends and drops, and as you draw near the light within your sixty feet of sight it ceases to be a mere glimmer. It is a man — stooped, shuffling slowly away from you down the tunnel, a guttering lamp swinging from one hand so that the light swells and fades with each step. He does not look back. He has not seen you, or does not care to.


