"Aye, Balder." Orrik's shoulders sag. "I heard the stone when it came down on him. He was behind me." He wipes a hand across his mouth. "I can walk. I'm not hurt — not in my body. Just tired, and thirsty, and afraid to be down here alone."

He lifts his lamp and looks at it, and when he speaks again his voice is low. "The lights look like men. Grey men in the old pit-gear, each one carrying a lamp like this one, but their lamps burn cold and give no heat. They don't speak. They just walk, down in the deep galleries, and if you come near they drift on ahead of you, as if they want you to follow.

"We saw them from the stope and followed — I don't know what we thought. Tarn swore he knew them. He called out old names, names of men who died down here a generation past, when the roof came down and they sealed the workings over the crew. The lights led us on from the stope, and Balder fell under the stone, and still they walked on. Tarn went after them, calling those old names, and he's down there still. I ran." His voice breaks a little. "I ran and lost my way, and I've been walking in the dark ever since."

He looks past you toward the way up, then back down into the black. "I can show you where the lights led us, if you mean to go for Tarn. But I'd sooner never set foot in that deep place again."


>>>>>> round 10, the player: "I won't drag you back into it, Orrik. Drink first," I say, handing him my waterskin if I have one, and offering him Balder's lamp since yours is guttering. "Tell me how far down the deep place lies and which way to turn, and then follow my rope back up to the mouth and wait for me by the timber. If I'm not out before the shift-bell, tell Hessa where I went."

  [time: 5 minutes later: Day 1, 6:00 PM]
