Orrik takes the waterskin and drinks deep, and some of the grey goes out of his voice. "Anvil's blessing on you," he says, and hands it back. He takes Balder's lamp from your hand, snuffs his own guttering one, and hooks it to his belt.

"Follow the gallery down the way I came," he tells you. "A hundred paces or so it comes to a crosscut, timbers in a square where the ways part. Take the left-hand drift, the one still going down. That runs to the old winding house — you'll know it by the fallen timbers and the great rusted drum where the rope once ran. Past that, the floor breaks and steps down into the deep galleries. There the roof has come in across the far chamber, and the dust lies white on everything. That's where the crew died, and that's where the lights led us. Tarn went in among the fallen stone, still calling those names. I didn't follow him that far."

He turns toward the rope, then looks back at you. "I'll follow your line to the mouth and wait by the timber, as you say. If the shift-bell rings and you're not out, I'll go to Hessa and tell her where you went, and what's down here." He hesitates. "Anvil keep you, Brakka." Then he starts back along the rope, his light shrinking up the tunnel behind you.


>>>>>> round 11, the player: I watch his light climb out of sight, then check the rope at my belt, shield up, and head down the gallery counting my paces to the crosscut. At the square of timbers I'll chalk a mark on the left-hand drift so I can find my way back, then go on toward the old winding house.

  [time: 15 minutes later: Day 1, 6:15 PM]

You watch Orrik's light climb the tunnel until it is gone, then check the rope where it is tied at your belt and go on down, counting your paces, shield up and eyes on the dark.

At a hundred and some paces the gallery opens into the crosscut Orrik spoke of: a square place shored with great timbers, where three ways part like the fingers of a hand. The dust here is troubled, many boots having passed and repassed. You chalk a clear mark on the left-hand drift, the one still running down, and take it.

The drift narrows and falls, the air growing still and cold and smelling of rust and old timber. Then the walls fall away and you come into the winding house. The roof is partly down, split timbers hanging like broken ribs, and across the floor lies the great winding drum, a barrel of rusted iron as tall as your chest, its rope rotted to black string. Here and there the fallen beams lie crossed, and the dust is thick, but through it boot tracks lead toward the steps down and back.

Beyond the drum, the floor breaks and steps down in rough stone into the deeper dark. Far below, in the black beyond your sight, a pale cold light glimmers and drifts — not one now, but two or three, moving slow and close together, as if walking.


>>>>>> round 12, the player: I stay low behind the winding drum, shield up, and watch the lights for a minute to see whether they're drifting toward the steps or staying in the deep. If they hold their distance, I'll chalk the top of the stair and ease down step by step, keeping my lantern shuttered and my eyes open for Tarn's tracks among the dust.

