You crouch low behind the winding drum and watch. For a full minute the lights do not come toward the steps. They hold to the deep, drifting slowly side to side, close together, as if three men walked a slow and aimless circuit far below. They neither gain nor fade.

You chalk the top of the stair and ease down, step by step, testing each stone, your lantern shuttered so that your own shadow gives you away to no one. The steps are rough and worn, and the dark presses in.

At the foot the deep gallery runs on, lower and colder than anything above, the roof so low your helm would scrape it if you stood too tall. The dust here is thick and white, and in it the boot tracks are plain: the miners going down, and one set turning back in a stumbling run. The three pale lights are still ahead of you, farther down the gallery, and now you can make them out for what Orrik said — grey man-shapes in the old pit-gear, walking slowly away from you, each with a cold lamp that gives no warmth, none of them turning to look back.

Among the tracks that go down, one set breaks away from the others and leads toward the far end of the gallery, where the roof has come in across the chamber and the white dust lies over everything.


