You chalk a mark at the mouth of the gallery and go on, hugging the wall. The figures do not turn — not at first. They drift ahead of you, and where the roof falls away and the chamber opens, they pass in among the fallen stone and stand there, three grey men with their cold lamps, in a loose ring around a fourth who kneels in the white dust.

You hear him before you see him plainly: a low, hoarse murmur, a litany of names spoken into the dark, one after another. It is Tarn. He kneels among the great fallen slabs where the roof came down, his back to you, his pick laid across his knees, and he is saying the old names over and over, as if calling the dead to answer. His voice is cracked and thin, and he seems to hear nothing but the names.

The three grey figures stand about him, still and silent, their lamps burning cold. None has turned to you — until you come to the edge of the fallen chamber. Then the nearest slowly turns its head your way, and its eyes are two points of cold grey light.


>>>>>> round 14, the player: I stop at the edge of the chamber, raise my shield, and speak low and steady in the Anvil Father's name. "Rest easy, you who were miners. I'm Brakka of the Anvil Father's temple, and I mean no harm. I've come only for Tarn, and to bring you the Father's peace."

I keep my longsword ready but lowered, and hold up my holy symbol with my free hand, watching whether the grey figure answers or moves toward me.

