The nearest figure does not advance. It regards you with its cold grey eyes, and after a long moment its mouth works, but no sound comes — only a slow rush of colder air across the chamber. The other two turn as well, and their lamps steady and brighten a little, as if the name of the Father were a thing they knew.

Tarn's litany stops. He lifts his head, and slowly, stiffly, he turns toward you. His face is grey with dust and his eyes are wide and strange, but there is recognition in them. "Brakka?" His voice is a scrape. "You came. But you mustn't take me yet." He looks back at the grey figures, and something softens in him, like grief. "They've been waiting down here since the roof came down. No one said the words over them. They sealed the workings and left them, and so they walk. So they walk." He shakes his head slowly. "I've been saying the names — all the names, so they'd know someone remembered. But I can't finish it. I don't know the words the Father wants."

He falls still, and the three grey figures stand waiting around him, their cold lamps burning, all their pale eyes now upon you.


>>>>>> round 15, the player: "I know the words, Tarn. I've spoken them over the dead at the temple gate for six years, and I'll speak them here." I sheathe my sword, kneel beside him in the white dust, and raise my holy symbol to the three grey miners. "Keep saying the names, friend, and I'll speak the Anvil Father's rite of rest over each of them: lay down your lamps and your picks, for the shift is done, and the forge-fire waits to take you home."

