The three of you take Balder's weight between you and carry him the rest of the way, out past the timber and into the open. It is full night now, and cold; the lights of Greyhollow burn small down the slope, and the stars are hard and close. You go down to the temple, and word runs ahead of you, and it is Hessa herself who comes to the door, wrapped in her old shawl, to find you with the two living miners on their feet and the third wrapped in wool.

She looks long at Tarn and Orrik, then kneels by Balder and draws back the wool a little. "The Father keep him," she says, very low. Then she looks at you, and something in her old face changes as her eyes fall on you — she sees the mark that was laid on you in the deep. "Brakka. What happened to you down there?"

And when you tell her — the grey men, the cold lamps, Tarn saying the names, the rite and the warm light and the way through the wall of stone — she is silent a long while. "Then the crew of the north workings are at rest at last," she says, "after a generation." She grips your arm. "And the Father has claimed you for his own work, Brakka. I see it. The dead will know your voice from now on."

She turns to the others. "The council can wait on their bricks. I will see to Balder's rites myself, and we will fetch the lamps and picks the dead laid down, and hang them in the temple, so no one forgets." Then to you: "Rest now. You have carried enough tonight. But first — come in, all of you, out of the cold."


