The mark does not change anything you can do — it grants no new feature, no spell, no bonus. It is a sign, not a power. What it looks like: your hands, and the holy symbol when you hold it, carry a faint lingering warmth, as of a forge only just banked. To ordinary eyes you look as you always did, though folk may notice you seem to bring the fire's warmth into a room. But to those with the sight — Hessa, the faithful, and, it seems, the dead — there is a soft light about you, like a lamp seen through dust. It is the Anvil Father's favour laid on you, and a truth about who you are becoming, not a strength you can spend.

Inside, the temple is warm. The great brazier by the altar has been built up, and the brothers and sisters set Balder on a low table in the quiet, washing the dust from his face and laying his pick across his chest, as is the old way, until the full rites can be said. A healer — an old dwarf named Merla, who has set more mine-broken bones than anyone in the vale — is bent over Tarn, feeling his wrists and looking into his eyes.

She straightens and speaks softly, so as not to disturb the men. "No fever in him, and no wound but hunger, thirst and three days of the dark. Warm broth and sleep will do more for him than I can. Watch him through the night, though — he has looked on things that leave a mark on the mind." Tarn and Orrik sit wrapped in blankets before the brazier, bowls of broth in their hands, their faces slack with a weariness that goes deeper than the body.
